; NEGATIVE_CACHE_LIKELY.
;
; RFC 2308 §5: a negative answer is cached for the lesser of the SOA minimum and
; the SOA's own TTL. This zone sets both to an hour, so a customer who adds the
; missing record still sees the absence for that long — long enough to conclude
; the fix did not work, short enough that nobody suspects caching.
;
; The counterpart is negcache-low.test at 60 seconds, which nobody notices and
; which must therefore produce no finding.
$ORIGIN negcache-high.test.
$TTL 3600

@       IN SOA  ns1.test. hostmaster.propgate.invalid. (
                1 7200 3600 1209600 3600 )
@       IN NS   ns1.test.

; An address and no TXT, so a TXT query is NODATA rather than NXDOMAIN: the name
; exists, the type does not, and the answer carries the SOA that says how long
; that will be remembered. Both findings arrive together, which is realistic —
; and an apex TXT here would make the answer positive and carry no SOA at all.
@       IN A    198.51.100.90
